<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nimba Range</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mount Nimba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nimba Range is a chain of mountains on the borders of Liberia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Côte d'Ivoire</a>, rising to 1,752 metres at Mount Nimba. Its rich iron ore was mined on the Liberian side by the LAMCO consortium from 1963 until the civil war, and the Guinean and Ivorian parts form a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage nature reserve.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nimba-range.jpg" alt="Nimba Range" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yakoo1986 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nimba_Range.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Nimba Range forms the southern end of the Guinea Highlands, beside the Toura Mountains. It is a narrow ridge about 40 km long running from north-east to south-west, made mostly of Precambrian granite and quartzite holding deposits of iron ore. The name Mount Nimba may mean either Mount Richard-Molard, the highest peak, or the whole range; other peaks, all in Guinea, include Grand Rochers at 1,694 m and Mont Sempéré at 1,682 m. About fifty springs rise in it, among them the sources of the Cavally, Cestos and Sassandra rivers.</p><p>About 3,000 mm of rain falls on the highest ground and 700 to 1,200 mm lower down, most of it between May and October. Above 600 m lie Guinean montane forests: lower montane forest, cloud forest above 900 m dominated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> plum, and grassland and montane savanna on the summits. Below 600 m they give way to Western Guinean lowland forest, and the range is surrounded by forest-savanna mosaic and savanna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The range is home to more than 2000 species of vascular plants, 317 of vertebrates, 107 of them mammals, and more than 2,500 of invertebrates. Its endemic animals include the Nimba viviparous toad, Lamotte's roundleaf bat, Myotis nimbaensis and the Nimba otter shrew, and its western chimpanzees use stones as tools. In 1943 the colonial government set up a Strict Nature Reserve in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a> part of the range and extended it into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> the following year. The area stayed protected after Guinea and Ivory Coast became independent, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> declared Mount Nimba a biosphere reserve in 1980 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> shortly afterwards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Mount Nimba Strict Nature Reserve of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a> covers much of the range, which BirdLife International lists as an Important Bird Area. Because its rapids and waterfalls isolate streams and have favoured the rise of new species, the WWF counts it as a distinct freshwater ecoregion, with endemic fish, frogs and the Nimba stream crab. Mining of its high-grade iron ore is the main threat to its landforms and wildlife.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nimba Range” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Firestone Tire and Rubber Company’s concession of 1926 created one of the world’s largest rubber plantations at Harbel, and rubber remained the leading export until iron ore mining began at Bomi Hills in the 1950s, followed by the Bong and <b>Nimba</b> mines.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
